    question: 'Why do CSS transitions sometimes fail to fire when toggling a class in React, and what are the modern options for animation?',
    answer:
      "A CSS transition animates a **change** in a computed value. If an element is mounted with its final class already applied (rendering `<Panel className='open'>` only once `open` is true), there is no previous style to animate from, so it appears instantly; similarly, unmounting removes the node immediately, so exit animations never play. Classic fixes: keep the element mounted and toggle the class, or mount in the initial state and apply the end state after the browser has painted (a double `requestAnimationFrame`), or use a library (Motion, formerly Framer Motion, or React Transition Group) that defers unmounting until exit animations finish. Modern CSS helps too: `@starting-style` defines entry styles for newly inserted elements, and `transition-behavior: allow-discrete` animates `display`. React 19.3 made **`<ViewTransition>`** stable: it uses the browser View Transitions API to animate enter, exit, update and shared-element changes for updates triggered by transitions (`startTransition`, Suspense reveals, `useDeferredValue`), with `addTransitionType` to pick direction-specific animations; urgent updates are not animated. Always honour `prefers-reduced-motion`.",
